            <ArticleTitle>Warfarin-associated intracapsular hemorrhage causing an acutely painful total hip arthroplasty: a rare complication of prolonged anticoagulant therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>668-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hemorrhage is the most prevalent adverse effect of oral anticoagulant therapy. The incidence of bleeding complications is related to the duration and range of therapy. A patient on long-term warfarin anticoagulation developed a spontaneous intracapsular bleed into a total hip arthroplasty that had been implanted 11 years before. Joint aspiration allowed early recognition with immediate resolution of symptoms. Diagnosis and treatment of this rare complication are discussed with a review of the literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
